{#- modules/guardrails/policy-as-code.j2
    Policy as code: where it came from, and the architecture that still
    describes every enforcement system built since.

    `pac_history()` and `do_it_statically()` are written for a general
    audience and name no standards vocabulary — deliberately, because the
    acronyms cost a room the thread and buy nothing they will use.

    `policy_architecture()` and `policy_architecture_today()` DO teach the
    XACML PAP/PDP/PIP/PEP roles, via the CNCF Kubernetes Policy Management
    whitepaper's framing, then re-map them onto the agentic coding pipeline.
    They are for a practitioner audience that wants the reference model; no
    deck currently imports them. Do not mix them with `pac_history()` in the
    same run — the whole point of that slide is that it avoids the vocabulary.

    Source: CNCF / Kubernetes SIG-Security, "Kubernetes Policy Management"
    whitepaper v1 — github.com/kubernetes/sig-security (see deck SOURCES.md).

    Written against the brand-pack primitive contract (see AGENTS.md): no hex
    colors, no brand class names. Import WITH CONTEXT:

      {%- from "modules/guardrails/policy-as-code.j2" import
          pac_history, policy_architecture, policy_architecture_today with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide,
    slide_heading,
    quote,
    card_grid,
    numbered_steps,
    pipe,
    callout
-%}


{%- macro pac_history() -%}
{#- The middle step of the three — "put the rules in the process" — has been
    moving in one direction for fifty years, and this is that direction drawn
    once. Kept deliberately free of standards vocabulary: the point is the
    arrow, not the acronyms of any particular generation.

    Rendered as a numbered vertical list rather than a horizontal pipe: five
    stages each carrying a sentence are unreadable squeezed into cards across
    the slide, and 1-2-3-4-5 states the ordering outright instead of leaving it
    to arrows. Title and stages only — the subtitle, the then/now stamps and
    the closing callout were all cut, since the order already says "this got
    earlier over time". -#}
{%- call content_slide("How governance used to work") %}
  {{ slide_heading("", "How Governance used to work") }}
  {{ numbered_steps([
    {"title": "Written down",
     "detail": "Binders, wikis, annual attestations. Checked by audit, months after the fact.",
     "accent": "neutral"},
    {"title": "Checked by a person",
     "detail": "A review board, a security sign-off, a gate someone has to walk your change through.",
     "accent": "info"},
    {"title": "Checked by the platform",
     "detail": "The infrastructure evaluates every request and refuses the ones that break the rule.",
     "accent": "secondary"},
    {"title": "Rules as code",
     "detail": "Rules versioned, reviewed and tested like software; enforced in pull requests and CI.",
     "accent": "primary"},
    {"title": "Checked in the loop",
     "detail": "The same rules, pointed at what a coding agent is about to do — before it does it.",
     "accent": "success"},
  ]) }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro do_it_statically() -%}
{#- Epigraph opening the policy-as-code section. Practitioners who
    disagree about nearly everything else agreeing on this one point is a
    stronger argument for deterministic checks than the speaker asserting
    it. Attributed to the panel rather than an individual: the line claims
    consensus, and the specific speaker is not identifiable from the
    recording. -#}
{{ quote("Everyone here agrees as much as we can do statically we should do statically",
         "Dex Horthy &middot; Geoff Huntley &middot; Ian Livingstone &middot; Greg Pstrucha",
         source="The Great Loops Debate &mdash; AI Engineer World&rsquo;s Fair, 2026",
         url="https://youtu.be/c35YoMdnI78") }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro policy_architecture(image_path="modules/guardrails/img/xacml-policy-architecture.png") -%}
{#- The XACML reference architecture, shown as the whitepaper draws it.
    The figure is black-on-white, so it sits on a light plate — that is a
    legibility requirement of the source image, not a brand colour. -#}
{%- call content_slide("Policy architecture") %}
  {{ slide_heading("", "Policy as Code") }}
  <div style="flex:1; min-height:0; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; padding:8px 0;">
    <img src="{{ image_path }}"
         alt="XACML policy architecture: users or systems call the Policy Enforcement Point, which reaches resources and consults the Policy Decision Point; the PDP draws attributes from the Policy Information Point and policies authored by the Policy Administration Point."
         class="plain"
         style="width:100%; max-width:1000px; max-height:44vh; object-fit:contain; background:white; border-radius:12px; padding:12px;">
  </div>
  <p style="text-align:right; font-size:11px; opacity:0.6; margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    Figure from <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes/sig-security/blob/main/sig-security-docs/papers/policy/CNCF_Kubernetes_Policy_Management_WhitePaper_v1.pdf">CNCF &amp; Kubernetes SIG-Security, &ldquo;Kubernetes Policy Management&rdquo; white paper v1 (p.&nbsp;6)</a>
  </p>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro policy_architecture_today() -%}
{#- The payoff for teaching 2003 vocabulary in 2026: the agentic pipeline
    is the same diagram with the boxes renamed. -#}
{%- call content_slide("Same architecture, new actors") %}
  {{ slide_heading("",
                   "Your agent pipeline is the same diagram",
                   "Rename the boxes and the twenty-year-old architecture describes this year's tooling.") }}
  {{ pipe([
    {"label": "Request",
     "detail": "A coding agent proposes a change or a tool call",
     "accent": "neutral"},
    {"label": "PEP",
     "detail": "The hook, the PR gate, the CI check that intercepts it",
     "accent": "warning"},
    {"label": "PDP",
     "detail": "Your policy engine — the rules that decide, deterministically",
     "accent": "primary",
     "note": "PIP: repo context, metadata, and history. PAP: the repo itself — policies versioned next to the code they govern"},
    {"label": "Allow / deny",
     "detail": "Merge, block, or escalate to a human — with the reason recorded",
     "accent": "success"},
  ]) }}
  {{ callout("The architecture is twenty years old.
              <strong>Only the requester is new.</strong>", accent="primary") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}
